#!/bin/sh
# CLASS-A HISTORY-REWRITE GUARD — #158.
#
# Refuses any NON-FAST-FORWARD move of a local branch in a shared working
# tree. That is the harm class observed in the libs/iridium incident: two
# seats writing one tree, one seat's amend destroying the other's commit,
# with NO PUSH ANYWHERE IN THE HARM PATH. Branch protection cannot see this
# class at all -- the damage completes before anything reaches a remote --
# so this hook is AUTHORITATIVE here, not advisory.
#
# WHY THIS HOOK AND NOT prepare-commit-msg
#
# The mechanism first ruled for #158 was `prepare-commit-msg` keyed on
# `$2 = "commit"`. Measured, that is holed:
#
#     git commit --amend            -> source=[commit]  sha=[HEAD]
#     git commit --amend -m "..."   -> source=[message] sha=[]
#     git commit -m "..."           -> source=[message] sha=[]
#
# An amend WITH `-m` is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a plain commit at that hook --
# and `-m` is the form scripts and agents use, which is almost certainly the
# form that caused the incident. A guard must fire on the incident that
# minted it; that one would have waved it through, for the second time.
#
# `reference-transaction` sees the REF MOVE itself, so the invocation form is
# irrelevant. It also survives `--no-verify` (which bypasses only `pre-commit`
# and `commit-msg`).
#
# ⭐ ONE PREDICATE, TWO SURFACES. "Old is not an ancestor of new" is exactly
# the predicate a remote uses to reject a force-push. Class A enforces it
# locally where the remote cannot see; class B enforces it at the remote where
# the local hook can be skipped. Same rule, two fault domains.
#
# ⛔ THE REPORTED `old` IS NOT THE REF'S OLD VALUE, AND MUST NOT BE USED
#
# git supplies the old OID on stdin ONLY when the caller stated an expected
# old value. Otherwise it sends all-zero. Measured on git 2.47.1:
#
#     git commit --amend                 old=REAL   (refuses correctly)
#     git reset --hard HEAD~1            old=REAL   (refuses correctly)
#     git branch -f <b> <earlier>        old=ZERO   <-- discards commits
#     git update-ref <ref> <earlier>     old=ZERO   <-- discards commits
#     git checkout -B <b> <earlier>      old=ZERO   <-- discards commits
#     git rebase                         old=ZERO   <-- discards commits
#     git branch -D <b>                  old=ZERO, new=ZERO
#
# The first version of this hook read all-zero `old` as "branch creation" and
# allowed it, so it waved through rebase, `branch -f` and `checkout -B` --
# and its own acceptance test passed, because amend and reset happen to sit
# in the half of the class where git does state an expected value. A SAMPLE
# OF THE WORKING HALF IS NOT THE CLASS.
#
# So the ref's real prior value is READ HERE, with `rev-parse`, which at the
# "prepared" state still resolves to the pre-transaction value. That removes
# the dependence on a field that is only sometimes populated, rather than
# trying to interpret it.
#
# ⛔ DELETION IS OUT OF SCOPE, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT AN OVERSIGHT
#
# `git pack-refs` prunes loose refs by emitting `refs/heads/*` deletions
# through this very hook, so a rule refusing deletion aborts `pack-refs` AND
# `git gc` with rc 128 in every flagged repo. Attempts to separate the two
# were measured and all failed:
#
#   - packed-refs membership: `branch -D` of a PACKED branch reports exactly
#     what a pack-refs prune reports, and additionally emits a deletion for
#     `refs/heads/main`, which nobody asked to delete.
#   - reachability of the tip from another ref: a single-branch repo running
#     `git gc` looks identical to a real deletion.
#   - the reported `old` field: incidentally differs, but it is the very
#     field proven unreliable above, and `update-ref -d <ref> <old>` is a
#     genuine deletion that states it.
#
# A CONTROL THAT CANNOT DISTINGUISH THE HARM FROM ROUTINE MAINTENANCE ON THE
# SAME SURFACE MUST NOT CLAIM THAT HARM. Breaking `git gc` would get this
# hook uninstalled, which is a worse outcome than the gap. Deletion is left
# to git's own `branch -d` unmerged-work refusal; `branch -D` remains
# unguarded here and is recorded as open in the #158 findings.
#
# SCOPE
#
# Fires only when this repository carries the claim ref
# `refs/guards/shared-tree` in its common ref store. Ruled at ablative/docs
# `f3016c7`, amended `15b0fd5`, `09740ee`, `63c6339`, `b63bf0d`.
#
# ⛔ THE CLAIM IS A FACT ABOUT A DEPLOYMENT, NOT ABOUT THE PROJECT: it
# asserts that THIS working tree, ON THIS BOX, is shared by more than one
# seat. A clone is a different working tree. It cannot inherit sharedness --
# only acquire it.
#
# ⭐ SO THE CLAIM MUST NOT TRAVEL, AND THE HOOK MUST. Those are opposite
# requirements and the earlier design ran them together: the marker was a
# TRACKED FILE, which made a local fact travel and handed every laptop clone
# a shared-tree guard for a tree nobody shares. That was a second defect, not
# robustness. A plain `git clone` does not fetch `refs/guards/*` (the default
# refspec is heads-only), and under this ruling THAT IS THE MECHANISM
# WORKING. Claiming a clone is an explicit act on arrival, done as part of
# provisioning.
#
# ⛔ MAKING THE CLAIM TRAVEL IS THEREFORE EXPLICITLY REFUSED. A mirror
# refspec or per-clone fetch config would resurrect claim-about-a-box-you-
# are-not-on AND add a manual step as a load-bearing dependency -- the same
# class as the stale absolute `core.hooksPath` below.
#
# ⛔ A tracked file also forked with every branch: two checkouts of one
# repository could disagree about whether the guard was claimed, while the
# refs they protect are shared. The ref is per-repository by construction,
# so no branch and no worktree can hold a different answer.
#
# ⛔ NOT A GIT-CONFIG KEY EITHER, and not for the reason this block used to
# give. A config key is per-repository too, but it is content-free and
# invisible to git tooling. The ref points at a blob recording seat, date
# and scope, so the claim IS its own citation: a reader who finds a repo
# guarded can ask WHO claimed it and WHY without leaving the repository.
# The ref is equally immune to the failure that motivated the old wording:
# it lives inside the repository, so it moves when the repository moves --
# unlike a stale ABSOLUTE `core.hooksPath`, which is what silently disabled
# hooks in four estate repos after directories moved.
#
# ⛔ WHAT THE REF DOES NOT CARRY IS ITS OWN REMOVAL. MEASURED 2026-08-08:
# default `core.logAllRefUpdates` writes NO reflog for `refs/guards/*`;
# `always` writes one and `git update-ref -d` DESTROYS IT along with the
# ref. Afterwards there is no ref, no `logs/refs/guards/*`, and no mention
# in HEAD's reflog. The blob's self-citation holds for a claim that is
# STILL SET and says nothing about one silently dropped. An earlier draft
# of this block said the ref "carries a reflog"; that was measured false
# and STRUCK at `b63bf0d`. It is recorded here rather than quietly deleted
# because A FALSE PREMISE IN A RULING IS A FUTURE LICENCE TO SKIP THE
# REMEDY IT WAS USED TO JUSTIFY.
#
# ⭐ THE WITNESS IS RELOCATED, NOT LOST. The legitimate disarm of a shared
# repository is AN EDIT TO THE MUST-BE-CLAIMED LIST in the estate ledger --
# a commit: visible, reviewable, attributable, exactly as the tracked file
# was. What IS genuinely lost is act-time detection: the list catches an
# unclaim at the next battery run rather than at the moment of the act, so
# the list's coverage and the battery's cadence are GUARD PROPERTIES now,
# not test hygiene.
#
# KNOWN GAP, STATED NOT HIDDEN
#
# This hook cannot defend a repo it is not installed in, and `.git/hooks` is
# not carried by a clone. Install via a RELATIVE `core.hooksPath` into a
# tracked directory (`git config core.hooksPath .githooks`) -- relative,
# because the four repos that broke did so by being ABSOLUTE. Installation
# remains a manual step and therefore remains the residual gap.
#
# ⭐ NOTE THE ASYMMETRY THIS SECTION EXISTS TO KEEP STRAIGHT: the INSTALL
# must travel (tracked directory, relative path) and the CLAIM must not.
# A change that makes one of them behave like the other is a regression
# even when it looks like an improvement.

set -eu

state="${1:-}"

# Only the "prepared" state can abort the transaction. "committed"/"aborted"
# are notifications and a non-zero exit there means nothing.
[ "$state" = "prepared" ] || exit 0

# THE CLAIM PREDICATE — sourced, never hand-copied. Ruling point 1: this hook
# and check_guard_active.sh shared a defect BY SHARING A PREDICATE THEY EACH
# KEPT BY HAND, so they now share one implementation by construction.
#
# ⛔ A MISSING PREDICATE FILE IS A REFUSAL, NOT A SKIP. Every other failure in
# this file's scope check exits 0, and that is correct for "the guard does not
# apply here". It is CATASTROPHIC for "the guard could not be loaded": those two
# states are indistinguishable to anyone downstream, and the second one is the
# guard being silently absent in a repo that asked for it. Absence ⇒ RAISE.
predicate_dir=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" 2>/dev/null && pwd) || predicate_dir=""
if [ -z "$predicate_dir" ] || [ ! -f "$predicate_dir/claim_predicate.sh" ]; then
    printf '⛔ REFUSED: the class-A guard cannot load its claim predicate.\n' >&2
    printf '   Expected: %s/claim_predicate.sh\n' "${predicate_dir:-<unresolved>}" >&2
    printf '   The hook is installed but INCOMPLETE — claim_predicate.sh must be\n' >&2
    printf '   tracked and shipped alongside this hook, or the guard cannot run.\n' >&2
    printf '   Refusing rather than skipping: a guard that cannot load is not a\n' >&2
    printf '   guard that does not apply, and only one of those is safe to ignore.\n' >&2
    exit 1
fi
# shellcheck source=claim_predicate.sh
. "$predicate_dir/claim_predicate.sh"

# Unclaimed repository ⇒ the guard does not apply here. Ruling point 3: this is
# the LEGITIMATE unclaimed state, not a defect.
claim_is_set || exit 0

# The all-zero OID marks "no value". Its LENGTH differs between sha1 and
# sha256 repositories, so it is detected by "contains no non-zero character"
# rather than by comparing against a hardcoded 40-char literal.
is_zero() {
    case "$1" in
        *[!0]*) return 1 ;;
        '') return 1 ;;
        *) return 0 ;;
    esac
}

refused=0
while read -r old new ref; do
    # `old` is deliberately unread -- see the block above. Named for the
    # protocol's shape, discarded on purpose.
    :

    # Local branches only. refs/remotes/* moves on fetch and is not our
    # business; refs/tags, refs/stash, HEAD and the rebase/merge pseudo-refs
    # are handled by their own rules.
    case "$ref" in
        refs/heads/*) ;;
        *) continue ;;
    esac

    # DELETION -- out of scope, measured, documented above. Allowed so that
    # `git gc` and `git pack-refs` keep working.
    if is_zero "$new"; then
        continue
    fi

    # THE REF'S REAL PRIOR VALUE, read rather than trusted. At "prepared" the
    # transaction has not been committed, so this still resolves to the value
    # the ref held before the move.
    current=$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$ref^{commit}" 2>/dev/null) || current=""

    # Ref does not exist yet: this is a creation. Nothing can be lost.
    if [ -z "$current" ]; then
        continue
    fi

    # Resolve the incoming value. A `new` that will not resolve to a commit
    # (a symref payload such as "ref:refs/heads/x", or a corrupt OID) cannot
    # be proved safe, and unprovable means refused -- the safe direction.
    target=$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$new^{commit}" 2>/dev/null) || target=""
    if [ -z "$target" ]; then
        printf '⛔ REFUSED: %s -- incoming value %s does not resolve to a commit,\n' "$ref" "$new" >&2
        printf '   so this move cannot be shown to preserve history.\n' >&2
        refused=1
        continue
    fi

    # THE PREDICATE. If the current tip is an ancestor of the new one, no
    # committed work is lost. If it is not, this move discards commits --
    # amend, reset backwards, rebase, branch -f, checkout -B. In a shared
    # tree there is no such move that is safe to wave through, because HEAD's
    # author tells you NOTHING about whose work it is (git identity does not
    # vary across seats on one box).
    if ! git merge-base --is-ancestor "$current" "$target" 2>/dev/null; then
        printf '⛔ REFUSED: %s would move NON-FAST-FORWARD in a shared working tree.\n' "$ref" >&2
        printf '   %s -> %s\n' "$current" "$target" >&2
        printf '   Commits reachable only from the current tip would be lost, and in a\n' >&2
        printf '   shared tree they may be another seat'\''s. A correction is a NEW COMMIT.\n' >&2
        printf '   If this is genuinely yours to discard, the sanctioned escape is to\n' >&2
        printf '   UNCLAIM the repository deliberately:\n' >&2
        printf '       git update-ref -d refs/guards/shared-tree\n' >&2
        printf '   ⛔ AND SAY SO OUT LOUD, because unlike the old marker THIS LEAVES NO\n' >&2
        printf '   TRACE -- no reflog survives the deletion, so nothing else will ever\n' >&2
        printf '   report that this repo was unguarded. If the unclaiming is permanent,\n' >&2
        printf '   it must also be recorded as an edit to the must-be-claimed list in\n' >&2
        printf '   the estate ledger; that edit is the commit that makes it reviewable:\n' >&2
        printf '       ablative/docs/tracking/must-be-claimed.tsv\n' >&2
        printf '   ⛔ AND NOTHING WILL CHASE YOU FOR IT. As of 2026-08-08 NO BATTERY\n' >&2
        printf '   RUNS check_must_be_claimed.sh anywhere in the estate, so an unclaim\n' >&2
        printf '   you do not record is not caught late -- it is not caught. The edit\n' >&2
        printf '   above is not paperwork behind an alarm; RIGHT NOW IT IS THE ONLY\n' >&2
        printf '   RECORD THERE WILL EVER BE.\n' >&2
        refused=1
    fi
done

exit "$refused"
